<<The Utility of Silence>>

* The error suppression operator is rarely essential because the main body of modern code can typically validate state before execution.
* One niche scenario where the operator might be used involves a system function where the error is injected into the body of the system response by lower-level C libraries.
* You should ensure the body of your logic remains clean by using explicit checks instead of suppressing warnings.
* Even if the body of your application is well-tested, race conditions between `file_exists` and `fopen` could still trigger an error within the body of the script.
* Developers generally avoid the operator because it can hide critical flaws within the body of the logic flow.
* If you must use it, verify that the body of the error handling block is robust enough to manage failure gracefully.

